    Section(
      title: '+ Explain cervical cord neurapraxia, the role of the Torg ratio, and the evidence regarding return-to-play after transient quadriplegia.',
      content:
          '**Cervical Cord Neurapraxia (CCN) — Transient Quadriplegia**:\n\n**Pathophysiology**:\n- **CCN** is a transient episode of bilateral sensory and/or motor dysfunction in the upper and lower extremities following cervical hyperextension, hyperflexion, or axial loading.\n- Caused by **transient compression of the cervical spinal cord** within a developmentally narrowed spinal canal.\n- The cord is temporarily compressed between the posterior vertebral body/disc and the ligamentum flavum, causing ischemia or mechanical distortion.\n- Symptoms resolve as the neck returns to neutral and compression is relieved — typically within minutes to hours.\n- **DISTINCT from a \'stinger\' (burner)**: Stingers are unilateral brachial plexus traction injuries (one arm only). CCN is bilateral with involvement of arms AND legs — this indicates spinal cord, not peripheral nerve, involvement.\n\n**Significance of the Torg-Pavlov Ratio**:\n- Ratio = sagittal spinal canal diameter / sagittal vertebral body diameter (measured on lateral X-ray or MRI).\n- **Normal**: >0.80. **Stenotic**: <0.80.\n- This patient: 0.70 — significant developmental stenosis.\n- **Limitations**: The Torg ratio has high sensitivity (~90%) but low positive predictive value (~12%) for CCN. Many athletes with low Torg ratios never experience CCN. The ratio alone does not dictate return-to-play decisions.\n- **Functional stenosis** (MRI assessment of CSF space around the cord) is more clinically relevant than the Torg ratio. Loss of CSF buffer (functional reserve) means the cord has less protection during dynamic motion.\n\n**Recurrence Risk**:\n- Athletes with one episode of CCN and developmental stenosis have a **56% recurrence rate** if they return to contact sports (Torg et al.).\n- This patient has had **TWO episodes** (one unreported) — his recurrence risk is even higher.\n- Each episode carries the risk of **incomplete recovery** — one episode may result in permanent spinal cord injury.\n\n**Return-to-Play Criteria and Controversy**:\n\n**Absolute Contraindications to Return (consensus)**:\n1. **Cord signal change on MRI** (edema, hemorrhage, myelomalacia)\n2. **Ligamentous instability**\n3. **Residual neurological deficit**\n4. **Recurrent episodes** (this patient — two episodes)\n\n**Relative Contraindications**:\n1. Single episode with complete resolution and normal MRI cord signal, but developmental stenosis persists (the canal does not change)\n2. Significant functional stenosis (reduced CSF space) even without cord signal change\n\n**For This Patient**:\n- Two episodes of transient quadriplegia with developmental stenosis (Torg 0.70) = **strong recommendation against return to contact sports**.\n- The risk of permanent spinal cord injury with continued play is unacceptably high.\n- This is a **career-ending recommendation** — one of the most difficult conversations in sports medicine.',
