    Section(
      title: '+ What additional evaluation is needed, and what are the activity restrictions and long-term management recommendations?',
      content:
          '**Additional Evaluation**:\n\n1. **Dynamic flexion/extension MRI or CT**: Assess for dynamic stenosis — the canal may narrow further with extension (the position of injury). This strengthens the case for disqualification if cord compression worsens with motion.\n2. **Somatosensory evoked potentials (SSEPs)**: Baseline SSEPs can detect subclinical conduction abnormalities even with a normal clinical exam. Abnormal SSEPs in a clinically normal patient indicate subclinical cord compromise and are an additional contraindication.\n3. **CT cervical spine**: Better osseous detail than MRI. Confirm no occult fracture or congenital anomaly contributing to stenosis.\n4. **Neurosurgical consultation**: For a second opinion on anatomic risk and to discuss whether surgical decompression could theoretically allow return to play. (Note: There is NO consensus that surgery allows safe return to contact sports with developmental stenosis.)\n\n**Activity Restrictions**:\n- **No return to football or any contact/collision sport**: This is the primary recommendation.\n- **Non-contact sports**: Permitted after full evaluation confirms no residual cord compromise. Swimming, cycling, weight training (with cervical-safe technique), running.\n- **Cervical strengthening**: Aggressive cervical musculature strengthening program — strong neck muscles provide dynamic stabilization. While this does not change bony anatomy, it reduces the risk during activities of daily living.\n- **Avoid high-risk activities**: No diving into water, no roller coasters, no trampolines, no wrestling. Anything that could cause sudden cervical hyperextension or axial loading.\n\n**Long-Term Management**:\n- **Annual follow-up**: Neurological exam and symptom review. Any new symptoms (hand clumsiness, gait unsteadiness, bowel/bladder changes) warrant urgent MRI to evaluate for myelopathy from progressive stenosis.\n- **MRI surveillance**: Consider repeat MRI in 2-3 years or if symptoms develop. Degenerative changes may worsen the stenosis over time.\n- **Cervical myelopathy screening**: Developmental stenosis at age 19 may lead to symptomatic cervical myelopathy decades earlier than the general population, especially with superimposed degenerative changes.\n- **Education**: The patient must understand the long-term implications — he has a narrow spinal canal that he will live with forever. Even a minor car accident or fall could cause cord injury that a person with a normal canal would tolerate without issue.\n\n**Academic and Career Planning**:\n- If he loses his athletic scholarship, the university\'s student-athlete services, NCAA insurance programs, and academic advisors must be engaged.\n- NCAA Catastrophic Injury Insurance may provide coverage if he is medically disqualified.\n- Career counseling — coaching, sports management, athletic training, broadcasting. Many former athletes find fulfilling careers in sports without playing.',
