    Section(
      title: '+ What systems-level challenges exist when a team physician recommends disqualification of a high-profile athlete, and how are athletes protected?',
      content:
          '**Systems-Level Challenges**:\n\n**1. Conflicts of Interest**:\n- **Team physicians** employed by or contracted with the university may face pressure (explicit or implicit) from coaching staff and athletic departments to clear athletes to play.\n- Financial incentives: A winning football program generates millions in revenue. Losing a starting offensive lineman affects the team\'s competitive position.\n- The physician\'s primary obligation is ALWAYS to the patient, not the team. This must be documented and understood by all parties.\n\n**2. Independent Medical Evaluation**:\n- For career-ending decisions, an **independent physician** (not employed by the university) should confirm the recommendation.\n- The athlete has the right to a second opinion. Encourage this — it protects both the athlete and the recommending physician.\n- Document that the recommendation is based on medical evidence, not opinion.\n\n**3. NCAA Protections**:\n- **NCAA Constitution**: Requires that the well-being of student-athletes is a core commitment.\n- **Medical disqualification policies**: Most conferences require that the team physician\'s medical decision is final and cannot be overridden by coaches or administrators.\n- **Scholarship protection**: NCAA rules generally protect medically disqualified athletes\' scholarships for the remainder of their degree. The university cannot revoke the scholarship because of the injury.\n- **NCAA Catastrophic Injury Insurance Program**: Provides coverage for athletes with life-altering injuries, including lost earning potential.\n\n**4. Documentation Requirements**:\n- Detailed medical record documenting: diagnosis, imaging findings, recurrence risk, evidence supporting the recommendation, discussion with the patient and family.\n- Written disqualification letter with specific activities contraindicated.\n- Documentation that the athlete was informed of risks and the basis for the decision.\n\n**5. Second-Opinion Seeking**:\n- The family may seek out physicians willing to clear him. \'Doctor shopping\' for return-to-play clearance is a known phenomenon.\n- If another physician clears him against your recommendation, document your disagreement and the basis for it. The liability risk shifts but does not disappear.\n\n**6. Younger Brother Consideration**:\n- The 16-year-old brother also plays football. Cervical stenosis has a heritable component. He should be screened with cervical spine imaging before continuing contact sports.\n- This is a family health issue, not just an individual one.',
